(6186268, 911387, 113419, '<td valign="top"><br/><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>My first impression was nice - but this needs more contrast.  I see from your comments that I''m not alone.<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>Your composition is good as the road and the power line poles lead us into the picture.  I think you chose well presenting this in black &amp; white; but again, with a little more contrast this would have been a much better photo.<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>Yes, you met the challenge head on<br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>Artisticly?  You cropped your photo very well and did a wonderful job capturing the leading lines.  <br/>Emotionally?  This photo draws out such a feeling of loneliness and from your title I believe that is what you going for.  You made a good capture of the clouds and that adds to the feeling.<br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>Has it been mentioned that you need more contrast?  ;-)  That would be the biggest suggestion for making this a better image.  Everything else has been done very well.  I see from your profile that this is your first entry.  You finished in the middle of the pack which is GREAT!  And if you can do this on your first try I can only imagine what else you have in store for us and I look forward to seeing it!  :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First Impressions: My first impression was nice - but this needs more contrast.  I see from your comments that I''m not alone. Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): Your composition is good as the road and the power line poles lead us into the picture.  I think you chose well presenting this in black & white; but again, with a little more contrast this would have been a much better photo. Challenge: Yes, you met the challenge head on Artistic/Emotion: Artisticly?  You cropped your photo very well and did a wonderful job capturing the leading lines.  Emotionally?  This photo draws out such a feeling of loneliness and from your title I believe that is what you going for.  You made a good capture of the clouds and that adds to the feeling. Summary: Has it been mentioned that you need more contrast?  ;-)  That would be the biggest suggestion for making this a better image.  Everything else has been done very well.  I see from your profile that this is your first entry.  You finished in the middle of the pack which is GREAT!  And if you can do this on your first try I can only imagine what else you have in store for us and I look forward to seeing it!  :-)', '2010-10-08 13:25:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(6186305, 911696, 113419, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>My first impression was this is by someone who isn''t from around here!<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>I think it''s just a little dark, but that''s not always a bad thing and I can see where it can work for this picture.  If your wanting to present a gloomy cloudy day.  You have good depth and focus.  The fence is good as a leading line but for some reason my eye is first drawn to the trees in the background.  As I look closer maybe that is because of that circle thing which I can''t really tell is attached to the fence or if it''s a hay ring in the background.<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>Yep, life in the country includes chickens and you''ve got those babies in there.  I''m glad to know (by reading your notes) that there are horses around there somewhere.<br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>Artisticly? It''s a good photo with nice leading lines.  I think color is a major aspect that is missing in this photo (as the comment just below says,...."too bad it wasn''t a blue sky.").  Of course we can''t control the immediate condtions under which we shoot, but we can go back are re-shoot.  Since color is missing maybe it would have done better as b&amp;w or sepia?   <br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>In my opinion there is no WOW factor here.  DPC voters like WOW!  Other than that it''s a nice photo.  :-)<br/> <br/>I see that you''ve entered two challenges thus far.  I hope you continue with here at DPC and I look forward to seeing more images from the Netherlands  :-)<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First Impressions: My first impression was this is by someone who isn''t from around here! Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): I think it''s just a little dark, but that''s not always a bad thing and I can see where it can work for this picture.  If your wanting to present a gloomy cloudy day.  You have good depth and focus.  The fence is good as a leading line but for some reason my eye is first drawn to the trees in the background.  As I look closer maybe that is because of that circle thing which I can''t really tell is attached to the fence or if it''s a hay ring in the background. Challenge: Yep, life in the country includes chickens and you''ve got those babies in there.  I''m glad to know (by reading your notes) that there are horses around there somewhere. Artistic/Emotion: Artisticly? It''s a good photo with nice leading lines.  I think color is a major aspect that is missing in this photo (as the comment just below says,...."too bad it wasn''t a blue sky.").  Of course we can''t control the immediate condtions under which we shoot, but we can go back are re-shoot.  Since color is missing maybe it would have done better as b&w or sepia?    Summary: In my opinion there is no WOW factor here.  DPC voters like WOW!  Other than that it''s a nice photo.  :-) I see that you''ve entered two challenges thus far.  I hope you continue with here at DPC and I look forward to seeing more images from the Netherlands  :-)', '2010-10-08 13:55:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(6186559, 911614, 113419, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>There is no way to tell you what my first impression was when I saw this photo.  It is just awesome!  All I could do was stare. <br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>Don''t you hate it when you miss lighting?  Especially lighting you KNOW is gonna make the perfect shot.  But WOW you ended up with this!  The sky doesn''t look drab at all.  I''m gonna have to check into some filters ;-)  <br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>Beautiful country side image; I''d say you met the challenge.<br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>Very artistic photo.  You captured perfect fall colors.  Of course you had to leave the fence post in the shot!  The fence is a wonderful leading line.  Heck the colors of the foloiage even lead you into the picture.  I love the way the fall colors play against that blue sky.<br/>This photo just has all that is fall - makes me want to saddle up and go for a ride.  And that looks like the perfect place for a ride.<br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>How do I summarize this?  It''s just a beautiful image.  Simply perfect!  :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First Impressions: There is no way to tell you what my first impression was when I saw this photo.  It is just awesome!  All I could do was stare.  Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): Don''t you hate it when you miss lighting?  Especially lighting you KNOW is gonna make the perfect shot.  But WOW you ended up with this!  The sky doesn''t look drab at all.  I''m gonna have to check into some filters ;-)   Challenge: Beautiful country side image; I''d say you met the challenge. Artistic/Emotion: Very artistic photo.  You captured perfect fall colors.  Of course you had to leave the fence post in the shot!  The fence is a wonderful leading line.  Heck the colors of the foloiage even lead you into the picture.  I love the way the fall colors play against that blue sky.This photo just has all that is fall - makes me want to saddle up and go for a ride.  And that looks like the perfect place for a ride. Summary: How do I summarize this?  It''s just a beautiful image.  Simply perfect!  :-)', '2010-10-08 19:34:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(6186584, 911102, 67697, '<td valign="top">Love how the trees connect the frames.  Great contrasts and a wonderful scene.  Congrats on the Red!</td>', 'Love how the trees connect the frames.  Great contrasts and a wonderful scene.  Congrats on the Red!', '2010-10-08 20:08:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6186867, 911073, 68586, '<td valign="top">Love the details in this - initially it almost looks like a simple silhouette - but the more you look the more there is.<br/> <br/>Very nice and congrats on your ribbon.</td>', 'Love the details in this - initially it almost looks like a simple silhouette - but the more you look the more there is. Very nice and congrats on your ribbon.', '2010-10-09 07:27:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(6186985, 911807, 43463, '<td valign="top">Wonderful lighting - love how the shadows and highlights of the light plays on the barn and the silo. You have some nice rich color tones (well balanced and not overdone in saturation) here with the deep, vibrant blue of the sky and the red of the barn that is very appealing to the eye. While the composition brings us up close and personal with the barn and the silo I think it might be ''too'' centered. I think that pulling back to have the barn and silo dominate the left bottom 1/4 of the composition while giving us a view of the expanse of rural/country landscape would further strengthen the composition''s feel of being in the country.</td>', 'Wonderful lighting - love how the shadows and highlights of the light plays on the barn and the silo. You have some nice rich color tones (well balanced and not overdone in saturation) here with the deep, vibrant blue of the sky and the red of the barn that is very appealing to the eye. While the composition brings us up close and personal with the barn and the silo I think it might be ''too'' centered. I think that pulling back to have the barn and silo dominate the left bottom 1/4 of the composition while giving us a view of the expanse of rural/country landscape would further strengthen the composition''s feel of being in the country.', '2010-10-09 11:20:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187115, 911102, 92577, '<td valign="top">Love the scene but wasn''t too keen on the heavy borders. Can''t argue with that great result though. WTG</td>', 'Love the scene but wasn''t too keen on the heavy borders. Can''t argue with that great result though. WTG', '2010-10-09 14:03:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187249, 911793, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Ja-9! Welcome to the critique zone...<br/> <br/>Overall, I would have to say that this shot does not match up to your usual standard. It doesn''t communicate to me that you were trying to get something specific across and the technicals distract from the photos aesthetic appeal.<br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>:  Your smallish depth of field focuses the eye on the right, but the actual focus isn''t as tight as I would expect - or very possibly you have a little shake from being in the canoe at such a relatively slow shutter for the focal length - can the D80 not push the ISO a little more? The lighting is quite stark and gives huge contrast between the trunks and the water which doesn''t really appeal to me.<br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>: There is some thirds action here, but not knowing what I am supposed to be seeing, it is difficult to use this effectively. I also suspect that the theme being ''country life'', the voters were expecting something more dynamic.<br/> <br/>Overall, it is a pleasant enough scene, but I am very confident of your ability to represent it more strongly.</td>', 'Hi Ja-9! Welcome to the critique zone... Overall, I would have to say that this shot does not match up to your usual standard. It doesn''t communicate to me that you were trying to get something specific across and the technicals distract from the photos aesthetic appeal. Technically:  Your smallish depth of field focuses the eye on the right, but the actual focus isn''t as tight as I would expect - or very possibly you have a little shake from being in the canoe at such a relatively slow shutter for the focal length - can the D80 not push the ISO a little more? The lighting is quite stark and gives huge contrast between the trunks and the water which doesn''t really appeal to me. Artistically: There is some thirds action here, but not knowing what I am supposed to be seeing, it is difficult to use this effectively. I also suspect that the theme being ''country life'', the voters were expecting something more dynamic. Overall, it is a pleasant enough scene, but I am very confident of your ability to represent it more strongly.', '2010-10-09 19:03:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187357, 911874, 113419, '<td valign="top"><br/><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>A lovely photo!  Very nicely done.<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>nice compostition, maybe not the sharpest of focus - not necessarily a bad thing in this situtation<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>A wonderful image for this challenge.<br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>A fine artistic job - love the way the road is just slightly off center - very good!  I like how you captured the light at the end of the road, so instead of just being lead into the photo we can actually see where we are going.<br/>On the emotional side, this is just perfect.  I can''t imagine anyone not seeing this photo without wishing they had this road to talk a walk on in the evening.  A very calming picture.<br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>Nice photo, one which I think most people can relate to.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First Impressions: A lovely photo!  Very nicely done. Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): nice compostition, maybe not the sharpest of focus - not necessarily a bad thing in this situtation Challenge: A wonderful image for this challenge. Artistic/Emotion: A fine artistic job - love the way the road is just slightly off center - very good!  I like how you captured the light at the end of the road, so instead of just being lead into the photo we can actually see where we are going.On the emotional side, this is just perfect.  I can''t imagine anyone not seeing this photo without wishing they had this road to talk a walk on in the evening.  A very calming picture. Summary: Nice photo, one which I think most people can relate to.', '2010-10-10 00:11:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187394, 911102, 30982, '<td valign="top">Awesome trees - very "Texas".  Congrats on a great shot (and knowing to convert it to B&amp;W to make it a great shot) and the ribbon!</td>', 'Awesome trees - very "Texas".  Congrats on a great shot (and knowing to convert it to B&W to make it a great shot) and the ribbon!', '2010-10-10 01:14:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187466, 911897, 113419, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>a shot of some cows in some beautiful country side<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>someone mentioned that the sky was overexposed - I myself don''t see it as overexposed but it''s hard to get the lighting just right when you mix shadows and bright light the way you have here; this could have been corrected by setting the shot up from a different angle so that the light and shadows aren''t competeing for attention<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>Your shot meets the challenge alright<br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>Artisticly this shot leaves me wanting more.  I understand what your going for....a nice landscape which included the cows.  I have problems capturing those shots too as mine always end up just looking like a snapshot.  I think that may be the problem here.<br/>Emotionaly, again here this shot leaves me wanting more<br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>A shot that meets the challenge but for a shot with more interest it may have benefited from being shot at a different angle....one in which the photo isn''t cut in half by the shadows and the sunlight....or one that concentrates more on the cows; maybe getting a closer of one and having the other slightly blurred in the background.  You say this was shot from the road.  Maybe next time the car could be parked and you could walk up to the fence?  More often than not (if your very still) cows will walk up close to the fence so if you move slowly this could offer you an interesting facial shot.  And as someone else mentioned there is that (distracting) fence post....just a step or two (or three) over could have corrected that.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First Impressions: a shot of some cows in some beautiful country side Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): someone mentioned that the sky was overexposed - I myself don''t see it as overexposed but it''s hard to get the lighting just right when you mix shadows and bright light the way you have here; this could have been corrected by setting the shot up from a different angle so that the light and shadows aren''t competeing for attention Challenge: Your shot meets the challenge alright Artistic/Emotion: Artisticly this shot leaves me wanting more.  I understand what your going for....a nice landscape which included the cows.  I have problems capturing those shots too as mine always end up just looking like a snapshot.  I think that may be the problem here.Emotionaly, again here this shot leaves me wanting more Summary: A shot that meets the challenge but for a shot with more interest it may have benefited from being shot at a different angle....one in which the photo isn''t cut in half by the shadows and the sunlight....or one that concentrates more on the cows; maybe getting a closer of one and having the other slightly blurred in the background.  You say this was shot from the road.  Maybe next time the car could be parked and you could walk up to the fence?  More often than not (if your very still) cows will walk up close to the fence so if you move slowly this could offer you an interesting facial shot.  And as someone else mentioned there is that (distracting) fence post....just a step or two (or three) over could have corrected that. ', '2010-10-10 09:36:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(6187562, 911073, 99981, '<td valign="top">This is truly amazing! The light is stunning. I added it to my favorites so i can stare it again and again.</td>', 'This is truly amazing! The light is stunning. I added it to my favorites so i can stare it again and again.', '2010-10-10 13:08:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188022, 911073, 98339, '<td valign="top">Well congrats to you!!!  I just got back to the internet today...excellent picture</td>', 'Well congrats to you!!!  I just got back to the internet today...excellent picture', '2010-10-11 01:12:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188024, 911102, 98339, '<td valign="top">super good job on the processing and lighting...love the deep rich tones...</td>', 'super good job on the processing and lighting...love the deep rich tones...', '2010-10-11 01:13:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188028, 911825, 98339, '<td valign="top">this is stunningly beautiful...I soooooo love Vermont...this is true "Americana at it''s best"</td>', 'this is stunningly beautiful...I soooooo love Vermont...this is true "Americana at it''s best"', '2010-10-11 01:14:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188437, 911310, 80908, '<td valign="top">Hi Jon, welcome to the ''critique zone''!<br/> <br/>My overall impression of your photo is that it is a pleasant scene but I think you could have shown it off better.  There is quite a lot going on, and changing your angle to use either the lane or the wires to lead the eye across the shot might make it easier to follow.<br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>: It''s an interesting choice of settings. Your lens is not at its best at f3.5, but equally you are not using a wide aperture to isolate your main subject by throwing the background right out. As a result it sits between the camps of ''lots of blur'' and ''deep depth of field''. I think that it would be better in the latter personally, so I would stand back a little, zoom in a little and throw the aperture up to about f7.1, which should bring the dog into focus. If necessary, you could boost the ISO, 400 is easily achievable on that body. For lighting, your subject is slightly backlit. I reckon a little fill flash (even from the onboard) would lighten this up a little, maybe give the eye some sparkle.<br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>:  You have used the rule of thirds well, but with all of those leading lines around, I would suggest a slight change of angle to use them to work across the diagonal of the shot.  You have also used a point of view looking down on your subject, which a way we often see animals and therefore struggle to get excited about. Perhaps kneeling or lying down to get a level or even upward shot would have produced something more interesting? I would probably go for level here. The aim is to make people see a familiar thing in a new way.<br/> <br/><b>PP</b>: A little use of the shadow/highlight tool might iron out some of the balance of dark and light, and tone down the harshness of the light on the grass. It also looks a little yellow, which some hue/sat could help with. I would be tempted to boost contrast a little with curves.<br/> <br/>In summary, this is a nice shot of a horse, but by playing with the angles, and deciding whether you want ''a horse'', or ''a scene with a horse in it'' and select your settings from there.</td>', 'Hi Jon, welcome to the ''critique zone''! My overall impression of your photo is that it is a pleasant scene but I think you could have shown it off better.  There is quite a lot going on, and changing your angle to use either the lane or the wires to lead the eye across the shot might make it easier to follow. Technically: It''s an interesting choice of settings. Your lens is not at its best at f3.5, but equally you are not using a wide aperture to isolate your main subject by throwing the background right out. As a result it sits between the camps of ''lots of blur'' and ''deep depth of field''. I think that it would be better in the latter personally, so I would stand back a little, zoom in a little and throw the aperture up to about f7.1, which should bring the dog into focus. If necessary, you could boost the ISO, 400 is easily achievable on that body. For lighting, your subject is slightly backlit. I reckon a little fill flash (even from the onboard) would lighten this up a little, maybe give the eye some sparkle. Artistically:  You have used the rule of thirds well, but with all of those leading lines around, I would suggest a slight change of angle to use them to work across the diagonal of the shot.  You have also used a point of view looking down on your subject, which a way we often see animals and therefore struggle to get excited about. Perhaps kneeling or lying down to get a level or even upward shot would have produced something more interesting? I would probably go for level here. The aim is to make people see a familiar thing in a new way. PP: A little use of the shadow/highlight tool might iron out some of the balance of dark and light, and tone down the harshness of the light on the grass. It also looks a little yellow, which some hue/sat could help with. I would be tempted to boost contrast a little with curves. In summary, this is a nice shot of a horse, but by playing with the angles, and deciding whether you want ''a horse'', or ''a scene with a horse in it'' and select your settings from there.', '2010-10-11 11:58:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188488, 911897, 113419, '<td valign="top">P.S. Please don''t hesitate to contact me if you have any questions!</td>', 'P.S. Please don''t hesitate to contact me if you have any questions!', '2010-10-11 12:52:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188501, 911404, 30861, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>I pretty well understand what you were after here, the sense of misty, otherworldly enchantment of a pasture in the early light. I applaud your ingenuity in experimenting with the filter. And the image definitely meets the challenge, "Country Life".<br/> <br/>The image as a whole, however, falls a little flat. Literally. There''s no sense of luminosity, and what mostly works well with misty scenes is that sense of inner luminosity, that eerie, glowing light. So it was a good experiment, but in the end it didn''t transcend, as it were.<br/> <br/>Some other reasons why:<br/> <br/>1: the composition is pretty static; one of the nice things about mist pictures is that they encourage a journey, visually, into the mist usually, and the very, very strong foreground anchoring of the horse precludes such a journey, there''s no BG glow or light to pulse us deeper.<br/> <br/>2: it''s unfortunate that the horse''s back is tangent to the fence line; crouching just a bit to make the shot would have solved this.<br/> <br/>So, overall it''s a pleasing shot but in the end it''s nothing more than that, no wow factor, no sense of involvement or mystery.<br/> <br/>R.<br/> <br/>ETA: FWIW, I happen to have a mist shot with softness and sharpness, mist and luminosity, foreground and depth, all integrated reasonably well, if you wish to look: it''s #4 in my portfolio. I have almost never pulled off a really nice mist shot, it is hard.</td>', 'Greetings from Critique Club  I pretty well understand what you were after here, the sense of misty, otherworldly enchantment of a pasture in the early light. I applaud your ingenuity in experimenting with the filter. And the image definitely meets the challenge, "Country Life". The image as a whole, however, falls a little flat. Literally. There''s no sense of luminosity, and what mostly works well with misty scenes is that sense of inner luminosity, that eerie, glowing light. So it was a good experiment, but in the end it didn''t transcend, as it were. Some other reasons why: 1: the composition is pretty static; one of the nice things about mist pictures is that they encourage a journey, visually, into the mist usually, and the very, very strong foreground anchoring of the horse precludes such a journey, there''s no BG glow or light to pulse us deeper. 2: it''s unfortunate that the horse''s back is tangent to the fence line; crouching just a bit to make the shot would have solved this. So, overall it''s a pleasing shot but in the end it''s nothing more than that, no wow factor, no sense of involvement or mystery. R. ETA: FWIW, I happen to have a mist shot with softness and sharpness, mist and luminosity, foreground and depth, all integrated reasonably well, if you wish to look: it''s #4 in my portfolio. I have almost never pulled off a really nice mist shot, it is hard.', '2010-10-11 13:13:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6188502, 911245, 113419, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>First Impressions: <br/>captured nice color here on the turkeys heads<br/> <br/>Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): <br/>Your compostion is fine but seems to me the background is very busy.  I realize it''s a wildlife shot and you can''t really set it up, but blurring your background some may have helped with this.  Also, as mentioned below getting rid of the road would be very benifical to this shot.<br/> <br/>Challenge: <br/>Your shot meets the challenge very well. <br/> <br/>Artistic/Emotion: <br/>Not sure this is a great artistic shot, looks more like good luck and as you mentioned in your notes you came over the rise and there they were.  Frankly I''m surprised they didn''t run away from you!<br/>Your title is the emotional kicker on this one.<br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>You lucked up and got a nice shot of turkeys!  :-)  BTW, I''m not knocking "luck" in anyway - I''m quite a fan of Lady Luck myself.<br/> <br/>EDITED TO ADD THAT THIS IS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-10-11 12:51:30.</i></td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  First Impressions: captured nice color here on the turkeys heads Technical (Comp, lighting, color, focus): Your compostion is fine but seems to me the background is very busy.  I realize it''s a wildlife shot and you can''t really set it up, but blurring your background some may have helped with this.  Also, as mentioned below getting rid of the road would be very benifical to this shot. Challenge: Your shot meets the challenge very well.  Artistic/Emotion: Not sure this is a great artistic shot, looks more like good luck and as you mentioned in your notes you came over the rise and there they were.  Frankly I''m surprised they didn''t run away from you!Your title is the emotional kicker on this one. Summary: You lucked up and got a nice shot of turkeys!  :-)  BTW, I''m not knocking "luck" in anyway - I''m quite a fan of Lady Luck myself. EDITED TO ADD THAT THIS IS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB', '2010-10-11 13:14:33.000000', '2010-10-11 13:51:30.000000', 0),
(6188612, 911897, 69008, '<td valign="top">A gradient ND filter would have helped to get some detail in the sky. You say you were driving when you shot this, so setup and forethought are necessarily minimal.  <br/>A touch up with levels, contrast, and saturation would help this quite a lot, to get the trees in the BG to show off their colors. <br/>Last, in the future, be more careful where you stick the fence posts.  : )</td>', 'A gradient ND filter would have helped to get some detail in the sky. You say you were driving when you shot this, so setup and forethought are necessarily minimal.  A touch up with levels, contrast, and saturation would help this quite a lot, to get the trees in the BG to show off their colors. Last, in the future, be more careful where you stick the fence posts.  : )', '2010-10-11 14:27:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(6192136, 911427, 98339, '<td valign="top">stunning is all I have to say</td>', 'stunning is all I have to say', '2010-10-14 23:37:11.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(6197011, 911655, 120755, '<td valign="top">Don''t understand the criticism below, truly beautiful to look at, keep on improving.</td>', 'Don''t understand the criticism below, truly beautiful to look at, keep on improving.', '2010-10-20 16:39:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(6199721, 911065, 118013, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by FrankRobinson:</b></div><hr/><i> Hi Michael, greetings from the Critique Club!  I hope you find something useful in this.<br/> <br/>My overall impression is that this is a very pleasant photograph of a beautiful spot. You have chosen a good time of day and the composition is very structured. The post processing seems a little heavy-handed though.<br/> <br/><b>Technically</b>:  You have achieved a good depth of field, and the focus is pretty sharp - although at this size (800px) the detail suffers - that is not something that you can do much about.  I am interested in your choice of aperture - f13 seems unnecessarily tight to achieve this - I would have thought f8 or even f7.1 would be enough. That would have allowed you to have a faster shutter - your current shutter speed is (for me) an odd choice: too slow to guarantee sharpness if windy, too fast to give the glassy effect on the water. <br/> <br/><b>Artistically</b>:  It is a very well composed shot, clearly utilising rule of thirds, with the sun well positioned and a nice diagonal from the boats. The trees are unfortunate, but nothing to be done about them. I would perhaps have cropped off the extreme right as the town over on the other side breaks up the peaceful line between the water and mountains.<br/> <br/><b>PP</b>:  Not a lot to say, but it does seem a little over-saturated, particularly the reds. Some of the boats have started to look a little ''blocky'', which I have noticed strong reds are prone to in digital, so I might have toned that down a little.<br/> <br/>In summary, this is a very pleasant shot of a pretty scene but is processed to be a little too punchy for the peacefulness I think you are going for. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>THANKS YOUR USEFUL COMMENT. ABOUT THE TREE, I JUST CAN NOT DEAL THAT VERY GOOD, U R RIGHT. BECAUSE I FIRST TIME WENT TO THERE FOR PHOTOGRAPHY, AND SPACE LIMITATION BUT IT NOT A EXCUSE FOR A GOOD PHOTOGRAPHER. <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-10-24 03:12:03.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by FrankRobinson: Hi Michael, greetings from the Critique Club!  I hope you find something useful in this. My overall impression is that this is a very pleasant photograph of a beautiful spot. You have chosen a good time of day and the composition is very structured. The post processing seems a little heavy-handed though. Technically:  You have achieved a good depth of field, and the focus is pretty sharp - although at this size (800px) the detail suffers - that is not something that you can do much about.  I am interested in your choice of aperture - f13 seems unnecessarily tight to achieve this - I would have thought f8 or even f7.1 would be enough. That would have allowed you to have a faster shutter - your current shutter speed is (for me) an odd choice: too slow to guarantee sharpness if windy, too fast to give the glassy effect on the water.  Artistically:  It is a very well composed shot, clearly utilising rule of thirds, with the sun well positioned and a nice diagonal from the boats. The trees are unfortunate, but nothing to be done about them. I would perhaps have cropped off the extreme right as the town over on the other side breaks up the peaceful line between the water and mountains. PP:  Not a lot to say, but it does seem a little over-saturated, particularly the reds. Some of the boats have started to look a little ''blocky'', which I have noticed strong reds are prone to in digital, so I might have toned that down a little. In summary, this is a very pleasant shot of a pretty scene but is processed to be a little too punchy for the peacefulness I think you are going for.   THANKS YOUR USEFUL COMMENT. ABOUT THE TREE, I JUST CAN NOT DEAL THAT VERY GOOD, U R RIGHT. BECAUSE I FIRST TIME WENT TO THERE FOR PHOTOGRAPHY, AND SPACE LIMITATION BUT IT NOT A EXCUSE FOR A GOOD PHOTOGRAPHER. ', '2010-10-24 04:10:53.000000', '2010-10-24 04:12:03.000000', 0),
(6244073, 911073, 34154, '<td valign="top">is that you sir? i look forward to country life.</td>', 'is that you sir? i look forward to country life.', '2010-12-04 02:29:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(6259886, 911504, 112972, '<td valign="top">Your pictures are great but they would do even better in the challenges if you make sure that you take advantage of the 800 pixel-long rule.  Remember you can go as long as 800 pixels along the longest side of your photograph.  Make them bigger and I am sure they would score higher.  When you save for web, if you are over 300 KB size, reduce the quality instead of the size.</td>', 'Your pictures are great but they would do even better in the challenges if you make sure that you take advantage of the 800 pixel-long rule.  Remember you can go as long as 800 pixels along the longest side of your photograph.  Make them bigger and I am sure they would score higher.  When you save for web, if you are over 300 KB size, reduce the quality instead of the size.', '2010-12-22 17:06:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(6276957, 911089, 1897, '<td valign="top">I like this, I the lighting is great and natural.  Not over-sharpened or overly contrasty like so many pics.  The only negatives are the saggy low heel boots that seem distracting and maybe the angle of her face.  I feel like I need to see more or less.</td>', 'I like this, I the lighting is great and natural.  Not over-sharpened or overly contrasty like so many pics.  The only negatives are the saggy low heel boots that seem distracting and maybe the angle of her face.  I feel like I need to see more or less.', '2011-01-09 00:41:25.000000', NULL, 0),
